<label>&#x02021;</label>Department of Endoscopy Center, Peking University Cancer Hospital, Beijing, PR China</aff><aff id="aff4">
<label>&#x000a7;</label>Department of Pathology, Peking University Cancer Hospital, Beijing, PR China</aff><aff id="aff5">
<label>&#x02225;</label>Key Laboratory of Carcinogenesis and Translational Research (Ministry of Education/Beijing), Department of Radiation Oncology, Peking University Cancer Hospital &#x00026; Institute, Beijing, PR China</aff><author-notes><corresp>
<email>zlwenaw@126.com</email>; <email>wangweihu88@126.com</email>.</corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>4</month><year>2023</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>29</day><month>6</month><year>2022</year></pub-date><volume>277</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>647</fpage><lpage>654</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 2022 The Author(s). Published by Wolters Kluwer Health, Inc.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2023</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an open access article distributed under the terms of the <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">Creative Commons Attribution-Non Commercial-No Derivatives License 4.0</ext-link> (CCBY-NC-ND), where it is permissible to download and share the work provided it is properly cited. The work cannot be changed in any way or used commercially without permission from the journal. <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ext-link></license-p></license></permissions><self-uri xlink:href="sla-277-0647.pdf"/><abstract><title>Objective:</title><p>To assess the efficacy and safety of intentional watch and wait (W&#x00026;W) and organ preservation surgery following neoadjuvant chemoradiotherapy plus consolidation CAPEOX in magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)-defined low-risk rectal cancer.</p><sec><title>Background:</title><p>Clinical T2/early T3 rectal cancers can achieve high yield pathological complete response (ypCR) rates after chemoradiotherapy; thus, an intentional W&#x00026;W or organ preservation strategy for good clinical responders in these subgroups can be further tested.</p></sec><sec><title>Methods:</title><p>This prospective, single-arm, phase 2 trial enrolled patients with low-risk MRI prestaged rectal cancers, who concurrently received chemoradiation, followed by four 3-weekly cycles of CAPEOX regimen. Following reassessment, clinical complete response (cCR) or near-cCR patients underwent W&#x00026;W/organ preservation surgery; the primary endpoint was a 3-year organ preservation rate.</p></sec><sec><title>Results:</title><p>Of the 64 participants, 58 completed treatment, with 6.4% and 33.9% grade 3 to 4 toxicities in the radiotherapy and consolidation CAPEOX phases, respectively, during a median 39.5-month follow-up. Initial cCR, and non-cCR occurred in 33, 13, and 18 patients, respectively. Of the 31 cCR and 7 near-cCR cases managed by W&#x00026;W, local regrowth occurred in 7; of these, 6 received salvage surgery. The estimated 2-year local regrowth rates were 12.9% [95% confidence interval (CI): 1.1%&#x02013;24.7%] in cCR and 42.9% (95% CI: 6.2%&#x02013;79.6%) in near-cCR cases, respectively. Eight patients received local excision, including 2 with regrowth salvage. Lung metastases occurred in 3 patients and multiple metastasis occurred in 1 patient; no local recurrence occurred. The estimated 3-year organ preservation rate was 67.2% (95% CI: 55.6%&#x02013;78.8%). The estimated 3-year cancer-specific survival, non-regrowth disease-free survival, and stoma-free survival were 96.6% (95% CI: 92.1%&#x02013;100%), 92.2% (95% CI: 85.5%&#x02013;98.9%), and 82.7% (95% CI: 73.5%&#x02013;91.9%), respectively.</p></sec><sec><title>Conclusions:</title><p>Chemoradiotherapy plus consolidation CAPEOX for MRI-defined low-risk rectal cancer can lead to high rates of organ preservation through intentional W&#x00026;W or local excision. The oncologic safety of this strategy should be further tested.</p></sec></abstract><kwd-group><title>Keywords:</title><kwd>organ preservation</kwd><kwd>chemoradiotherapy</kwd><kwd>neoadjuvant</kwd><kwd>rectal cancer</kwd><kwd>watch and wait</kwd></kwd-group><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>SDC</meta-name><meta-value>T</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>OPEN-ACCESS</meta-name><meta-value>TRUE</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta></front><body><p>Neoadjuvant chemoradiotherapy (NCRT) for locally advanced rectal cancer can improve local control and induce pathological complete response (ypCR) in some patients.<xref rid="R1" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>,<xref rid="R2" ref-type="bibr">2</xref> For patients who achieve a clinical complete response (cCR), a watch-and-wait (W&#x00026;W) policy can gain a significantly higher organ preservation rate (OPR) but with similar oncologic outcomes as in ypCR cases after total mesorectal excision (TME).<xref rid="R3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="R9" ref-type="bibr">9</xref> Thus, the W&#x00026;W policy is considered an attractive treatment choice to avoid major surgery and permanent stoma, especially for distal rectal cancers.</p><p>The cT2 or early cT3-substage rectal cancers, as assessed by magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), have very good oncologic outcomes when treated with TME alone.<xref rid="R10" ref-type="bibr">10</xref> However, this subgroup of patients has increased radiosensitivity to NCRT and can achieve ypCR rates of 35% to 50%, which are much higher than those of locally advanced tumors.<xref rid="R10" ref-type="bibr">10</xref>,<xref rid="R11" ref-type="bibr">11</xref> A retrospective analysis showed that extended NCRT for cT2N0 patients can achieve a very high initial cCR rate of 86%, thereby theoretically facilitating the intentional W&#x00026;W approach.<xref rid="R10" ref-type="bibr">10</xref>
</p><p>We designed this prospective phase 2 trial to test our hypothesis that an intensive NCRT regimen for MRI-defined low-risk rectal cancer can achieve high cCR and organ preservation rates through intentional W&#x00026;W and organ preservation surgery. We also aimed to observe the oncologic results of this strategy.</p><sec sec-type="methods"><title>METHODS</title><sec><title>Study Design</title><p>This phase 2, single-arm trial (PKUCH-R01) enrolled patients who were referred for treatment at a high-volume cancer center (Peking University Cancer Hospital). Patients with low-risk rectal cancer and the following baseline clinicopathological features were selected for inclusion: clinical T2/T3a/T3b rectal adenocarcinoma staged by T2/diffusion-weighted imaging (DWI) MRI, without the involvement of mesorectal fascia or positive extramural vascular invasion, with histologically confirmed grade 1 to 3 differentiated adenocarcinoma, and located within 8 and 4&#x000a0;cm from the anal verge or the anorectal junction, respectively, as confirmed on MRI. Other inclusion criteria were as follows: age greater than or equal to 18 years and less than or equal to 85 years; Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) performance status 0 to 1; no evidence of distant metastases on baseline computed tomography (CT) or positron emission tomography-CT scan; no evidence of positive lateral pelvic lymph node; no prior pelvic radiation therapy; no prior chemotherapy or surgery for rectal cancer; no active infections requiring systemic antibiotic treatment; absolute neutrophil count &#x0003e;1.5&#x000a0;cells/mm<sup>3</sup>, hemoglobin &#x0003e;10.0&#x000a0;g/dL, platelets &#x0003e;100,000/mm<sup>3</sup>, total bilirubin &#x02264;1.5&#x000d7; the upper limit of normal (ULN), aspartate aminotransferase &#x02264;3&#x000d7; ULN, and alanine aminotransferase &#x02264;3&#x000d7; ULN. The exclusion criteria were as follows: recurrent rectal cancer; serum creatinine &#x02265;1.5&#x000d7; ULN; inability to undergo MRI; history of prior malignancy within the past 5 years, except for adequately treated basal cell or squamous cell skin cancer, early-stage breast cancer, thyroid cancer, or small cell renal cancer; and a history of any arterial thrombotic event within the preceding 6 months. To allow for patient enrollment in accordance with clinical practice, we undertook a protocol amendment to: (1) exclude patients with grade 4 differentiation after the enrollment of 1 such patient because the reassessment of mucinous change is difficult; (2) allow enrollment of elderly patients and patients with a history of well-treated cancer with a good prognosis; (3) remove the baseline N status requirement because of the limited accuracy of MRI on N staging; and (4) make the completion TME an optional recommendation for patients with T2Nx tumor after local excision (LE) without positive margins. The protocol and later amendments were approved by the ethics committee of Peking University Cancer Hospital on August 6, 2016, and on December 17, 2018, respectively. All patients signed informed consent of study participation before treatment.</p><p>The overall consort flow diagram of patient recruitment, initial response, and treatment is showed in Figure <xref rid="F1" ref-type="fig">1</xref>.</p><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>FIGURE 1</label><caption><p>Overall consort flow diagram to show patient recruitment, initial response, tailored treatment, and organ preservation.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="sla-277-0647-g001" position="float"/></fig></sec><sec><title>Total Neoadjuvant Treatment (TNT)</title><p>All patients were treated with intensity-modulated radiation therapy (IMRT) using a concomitant boost technique by the Varian Rapid Arc system; the technical details and volume definitions are the same as in our previous reports.<xref rid="R12" ref-type="bibr">12</xref> The gross tumor volume and clinical tumor volume were 50.6 and 41.8&#x000a0;Gy, respectively, administered in 22 fractions; the biological equivalent dose was 48.4&#x000a0;Gy, as determined by a linear-quadratic formula. Oral capecitabine 825&#x000a0;mg/m<sup>2</sup> was concurrently administered with IMRT twice per day. The treatment duration of IMRT was 30 days. Patients were scheduled, at 2 to 4 weeks after the last fraction, to receive four 3-week cycles of consolidation CAPEOX (oxaliplatin&#x02013;capecitabine regimen), with each cycle comprising intravenous oxaliplatin (130&#x000a0;mg/m<sup>2</sup> on day 1 of each cycle) and oral capecitabine (1000&#x000a0;mg/m<sup>2</sup> twice daily on days 1&#x02013;14 of each cycle). Acute toxicity was evaluated according to Common Terminology Criteria for Adverse Events (CTCAE), version 4.0 every week during treatment. The decision for the adjustment or interruption of chemotherapy in cases with severe toxicity was made by the treating physician.</p><sec><title>Reassessment and Decision-making</title><p>The final evaluation of tumor response was undertaken 16 to 20 weeks after completion of the TNT and included T2/DWI MRI, endoscopic assessment, clinical examination, and serum CEA measurement. Moreover, chest and abdominal CT was used to check for distant metastases. The definitions of cCR/near-cCR/non-cCR were based on the Memorial Sloan-Kettering Cancer Center criteria.<xref rid="R13" ref-type="bibr">13</xref> A typical baseline or restaging MRI and endoscopic view of response is shown in Figure <xref rid="F2" ref-type="fig">2</xref>. Endoscopic biopsy was not routinely performed and was undertaken only when there was a deep ulcer or a superficial mucosal lesion. Each case included in this study was discussed by a multidisciplinary team (MDT) before further decision-making, and patients with cCR and non-cCR were primarily recommended to receive a W&#x00026;W management and TME, respectively. Patients with near-cCR tumors were recommended to undergo W&#x00026;W or receive LE; very small non-cCR cases were also allowed to receive LE. The final personalized management strategy was based on the decision from the MDT discussion, surgeons&#x02019; and patients&#x02019; preferences.</p><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>FIGURE 2</label><caption><p>Typical images of baseline MRI, restaging MRI, and endoscopic features of patients with different responses, assessed by Memorial Sloan-Kettering Cancer Center criteria through MDT discussion at Peking University Cancer Hospital. A1&#x02013;A3: One cCR case treated with W&#x00026;W, without regrowth; A1, baseline mrT3a tumor; A2, minimal hypointense fibrosis confined in the rectal wall by restaging MRI; A3, typical endoscopic view of cCR with significant flat/whitening mucosa and telangiectasia. B1&#x02013;B3: One near-cCR case treated with W&#x00026;W, without regrowth; B1, baseline mrT3a tumor; B2, mostly full-thickness hypointense fibrosis confined in the rectal wall by restaging MRI; B3, typical endoscopic view of near-cCR with minor mucosal abnormality and mild erythema. C1&#x02013;C3: One non-cCR case treated with LAR; C1, baseline mrT3a tumor; C2, intermediate signal in fibrosis beyond the rectal wall; C3, macroscopic view of palpable ulcerative lesion in rectum specimen. D1&#x02013;D3: One near-cCR case treated with LE, without local recurrence; D1, baseline mrT3b tumor; D2, mostly hypointense signal in fibrosis, slightly beyond the rectal wall; C3, macroscopic view of the specimen after pyramid LE with partial mesorectum. Pathology revealed ypT2 tumor with negative radial and deep margins. E1&#x02013;E3: One cCR case with regrowth treated with LE without local recurrence; E1, baseline mrT2 tumor; E2, MRI features of regrowth, intermediate signal in fibrosis confined in the rectal wall; E3, endoscopic view of regrowth (by narrow-band imaging), visible intraluminal lesion with positive biopsy. Pathology revealed ypT2 tumor with negative radial and deep margins.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="sla-277-0647-g002" position="float"/></fig><p>The following criteria should be met in choosing LE: (1) magnetic resonance restaging of &#x02264;T2N0; (2) lesion diameter of &#x02264;3&#x000a0;cm and a 3&#x000a0;mm negative radial margin; (3) LE should focus on tumor location and the risk of severe complications, like anterior dehiscence or rectovaginal fistula. (4) surgery should be performed by senior surgeons with experience of LE after NCRT; (5) LE should be done with caution in patients with broad line sphincter preservation when completion TME is necessary. Conventional transanal excision by retractor or transanal minimally invasive surgery technique were used to perform a full-thickness rectal wall resection or a pyramid excision with partial mesorectum. For patients with high-risk factors such as poor differentiation, positive lymphovascular or neural invasion, or positive radial/deep margin after LE, a completion TME was recommended. However, for patients with T2Nx and negative margins but without risk factors after LE, the protocol permitted surveillance and follow-up for patients with a low expected rate of residual tumor in completion TME specimen. The postoperative complications were graded using the Clavien-Dindo classification. Patients who underwent surgery did not receive any adjuvant chemotherapy.</p></sec></sec><sec><title>Surveillance and Management of Local Regrowth</title><p>Patients in the W&#x00026;W group or those who refused surgery were followed up with clinical examinations, endoscopies, T2/DWI MRI, and routine blood tests, including serum CEA measurement, every 3 months for the first year, every 3 to 6 months in the second year, every 6 months in the third year, and annually in the fourth and fifth years. The surveillance schedule is shown in Appendix Table a1 (supplemental file), Supplemental Digital Content 1, <ext-link xlink:href="http://links.lww.com/SLA/D907" ext-link-type="uri">http://links.lww.com/SLA/D907</ext-link>. When local regrowth was diagnosed, mainly by endoscopic findings or a digital examination, the patients were additionally reviewed by an MDT for treatment decision-making. Salvage TME and LE were both allowed for patients with local regrowth, depending on the location, size, and T stage of the lesion. Patients who had undergone TME or LE used the common follow-up plan defined in the National Comprehensive Cancer Network guideline.<xref rid="R14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>
</p></sec><sec><title>Endpoints</title><p>The primary endpoint was the 3-year OPR, which was defined as the percentage of patients who achieved cCR or near-cCR after neoadjuvant treatment, followed by LE or W&#x00026;W, and avoided TME. The duration of organ preservation was measured from the date of treatment initiation. Events include rectum resection by TME or death from any cause. The OPR would be estimated through the TME-free survival curve.<xref rid="R5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>
</p><p>The secondary endpoints included (1) non-regrowth disease-free survival (NR-DFS), which was defined as the time from treatment until death (any cause), tumor relapse (including local pelvic recurrence following TME or LE), any distant metastasis during or after treatment and residual tumor when the patient refused surgery; salvageable local regrowth following W&#x00026;W strategy was not included as an oncologic event when calculating the NR-DFS<xref rid="R5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>,<xref rid="R15" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>; (2) stoma-free survival, defined as the length of time wherein patients lived without a temporary or permanent stoma, was measured from the date of treatment initiation; the events for stoma-free survival included temporary stoma with the inability to reverse, permanent stoma, or death; and (3) major adverse events (adverse events were graded using the NCI Common Terminology Criteria for Adverse Events [CTCAE] Version 4.0).</p><p>The time of local regrowth was calculated from the date of the completion of neoadjuvant treatment in cCR or near-cCR patients who received W&#x00026;W. Cancer-specific survival was also analyzed, defined as the time from the date of completion of neoadjuvant treatment to death due to primary cancer or related to treatment.</p></sec><sec><title>Statistical Analysis</title><p>The baseline data analysis showed that 48% of locally advanced rectal cancers could be downstaged to stage ypT0-2N0 following 22f-IMRT with concurrent capecitabine. We assumed that at least 24% of locally advanced rectal cancers could be candidates for post-IMRT LE or W&#x00026;W and that the OPR would increase to 40% in low-risk rectal cancers that were treated by LE or W&#x00026;W following IMRT plus consolidation CAPEOX. Based on a superiority design, this study needed to recruit 64 patients to test this hypothesis, with 85% power (exact binomial test for proportions, &#x003b1;=5%, 1-sided). We anticipated &#x0223c;10% loss to follow-up and, therefore, planned to recruit additional 8 patients or close recruitment after 64 eligible patients had been enrolled. Most of the data are descriptive because of the single-arm nature of this prospective study. We estimated the cumulative incidences of local regrowth, NR-DFS, TME-free survival, and stoma-free survival with the Kaplan-Meier (KM) method to depict the time-to-event parameters. All statistical analyses were performed in SPSS for Macintosh, IBM SPSS statistics for Macintosh, Version 20.0 (IBM Corp., Armonk, NY). This study is registered at ClinicalTrials.gov with the identifier NCT02860234.</p></sec></sec><sec sec-type="results"><title>RESULTS</title><sec sec-type="subjects"><title>Patient Characteristics</title><p>Patient recruitment commenced on August 6, 2016, and ended on November 30, 2019. We recruited 68 patients, and 64 were eligible for inclusion in the study. The study reached the expected sample size. Data collection was undertaken in December 2021, with a median follow-up time of 39.5 months (interquartile range: 4.3&#x02013;67.8 months). The baseline information of the patient and tumor staging is shown in Table <xref rid="T1" ref-type="table">1</xref>.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="T1"><label>TABLE 1</label><caption><p>Patient, Tumor, and Treatment Characteristics</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1"/><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1">All Patients (N=64)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Age</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">58 (34&#x02013;85)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="2">Sex</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Male</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">42</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Female</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">22</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Distance from anal verge (cm)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4&#x000b7;0 (1.0&#x02013;8.0)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Distance from anorectal junction (cm)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.0 (&#x02212;1.0 to 5.5)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="2">Baseline magnetic resonance stages</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;T2N0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">11</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;T3aN0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">15</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;T3bN0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">6</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;T2N+</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">4</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;T3aN+</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">11</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;T3bN+</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">17</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="2">Tumor differentiation</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Grade 1</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">13</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Grade 2</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">48</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Grade 3</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">2</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Grade 4</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="2">Radiotherapy according to protocol</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Yes</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">64</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;No</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="2">Full 4 cycles of consolidation CAPEOX</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;Yes</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">58</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02003;No</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">6</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn fn-type="other"><p>Data are presented as number or median (interquartile range).</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></sec><sec><title>Toxicities</title><p>All patients completed the planned radiotherapy; 56 (87.5%) patients completed 4 cycles of CAPEOX chemotherapy, including appropriate dose reduction. Among the remaining 8 patients who had cycle or dose reduction, 2 received 1 cycle of CAPEOX and 3 cycles of capecitabine; 2 patients each received 3 and 2 cycles of CAPEOX; and 2 patients did not receive consolidation chemotherapy. The acute toxicities of chemoradiotherapy and consolidation CAPEOX are shown in Table <xref rid="T2" ref-type="table">2</xref>. No patient death occurred in the neoadjuvant phase of treatment.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="T2"><label>TABLE 2</label><caption><p>Acute Toxicities During Neoadjuvant Treatment</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="3" axis="1">IMRT Phase (N=64)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="3" axis="1">Consolidation CAPEOX Phase (N=62)</th></tr><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1">Events</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1">Grade 0&#x02013;2</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1">Grade 3</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1">Grade 4</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1">Grade 0&#x02013;2</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1">Grade 3</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1">Grade 4</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Nausea or vomiting</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">16</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02014;</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">27</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">4</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02014;</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Diarrhea</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">5</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">12</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">2</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Proctitis, rectal bleeding</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">36</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Anemia</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">12</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Leukopenia</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">26</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">40</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">5</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Neutropenia</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">10</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">25</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">3</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Thrombocytopenia</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">2</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">20</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">8</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Fatigue</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">29</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02014;</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">33</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02014;</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Dermatitis</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">19</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Hand&#x02013;foot syndrome</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02014;</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">8</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">2</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02014;</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Neurological toxicity</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">43</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Urinary disorder</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">20</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Others</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">6</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">27</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Overall (rate) [n (%)]</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">60 (95.7)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4 (6.3)</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">41 (66.1)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">20 (32.3)</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 (1.6)</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn fn-type="other"><p>No grade 5 events were reported in either treatment phase.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></sec><sec><title>Initial Response and Strategy</title><p>After completion of neoadjuvant treatment, mostly at 16 to 20 weeks following the delivery of the last fraction of IMRT, a MDT evaluated the status of the response to treatment. We initially identified 33 (51.6%) cCR, 13 (20.3%) near-cCR, and 28 (28.1%) non-cCR cases. All cCR or near-cCR cases had major regression that was determined on MRI and typical endoscopic features; no patient had residual tumor cells, as confirmed in endoscopic biopsies (if necessary). The majority of non-cCR cases had pathological evidence of residual tumor cells or were considered non-cCR, due to the presence of markedly deep ulcers or visible tumor on endoscopy, palpable nodule on digital examination, or significant incomplete regression observed on MRI.</p><p>The initial cCR, near-cCR, and non-cCR rates were 66.7% (10/15), 20.0% (3/15), and 13.3% (2/15) in T2 and 46.9% (23/49), 20.4% (10/49), and 32.7% (16/49) in T3a/b tumors, respectively, without significant difference (<italic toggle="yes">P</italic>=0.30).</p></sec><sec><title>Outcomes of Intentional W&#x00026;W or Organ Preservation Surgery</title><p>In accordance with the protocol, individualized treatment plans were recommended to patients, after the initial treatment decision-making based on the MDT&#x02019;s discussion. Accordingly, 31 of 33 (93.9%) cCR and 7 of 13 (53.8%) near-cCR patients underwent W&#x00026;W. Six patients (1 cCR, 3 near-cCR, and 2 non-cCR) received initial LE without positive margins and did not receive completion TME and, among them, 2 patients had a ypCR. Twelve patients (1 cCR, 3 near-cCR, and 8 non-cCR) received initial low anterior resection (LAR) and, among them, 4 patients had a ypCR. One patient died of a postoperative abdominal infection following a LAR. Five non-cCR patients underwent initial abdominoperineal resection (APR); and 3 non-cCR patients initially refused surgery.</p><p>A total of 7 regrowths occurred in 38 cCR/near-cCR patients who received W&#x00026;W, with a median follow-up time of 31.6 months (range 21.7&#x02013;57.1 months) since cCR/near-cCR achievement. All regrowths were intraluminal lesions that were detected on clinical examination or endoscopy, and all occurred within the first year after completion of neoadjuvant treatment. The estimated 2-year local regrowth rates were 12.9% [95% confidence interval (CI): 1.1%&#x02013;24.7%] in cCR, 42.9% (95% CI: 6.2%&#x02013;79.6%) in near-cCR cases, and 18.4% (95% CI: 6.1%&#x02013;30.7%) in cCR/near-cCR cases, respectively.</p><p>The KM curve of local regrowth in cCR/near-cCR patients following W&#x00026;W is shown in Figure <xref rid="F3" ref-type="fig">3</xref>.</p><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>FIGURE 3</label><caption><p>The KM curve of local regrowth in cCR/near-cCR cases following W&#x00026;W. The estimated 2-year local regrowth rates were 12.9% (95% CI: 1.1%&#x02013;24.7%) in cCR, 42.9% (95% CI: 6.2%&#x02013;79.6%) in near-cCR cases, and 18.4% (95% CI: 6.1%&#x02013;30.7%) in cCR/near-cCR cases.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="sla-277-0647-g003" position="float"/></fig><p>Six of the 7 patients with regrowths were curatively salvaged by R0 resections: 2 by LE, 3 by LAR, and 1 by APR. Another patient refused surgery after local regrowth and died of acute myocardial infarction.</p><p>Notably, delayed cCR occurred in 2 non-cCR patients who refused initial surgery during follow-up (64.2 and 54.4 months, respectively). No regrowth occurred in these patients and 1 patient had persistent grade 2 to 3 adenoma close to the scar of primary cancer.</p><p>The initial/sustained response and following management are shown in Table <xref rid="T3" ref-type="table">3</xref>.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="T3"><label>TABLE 3</label><caption><p>Initial/Sustained Response and Management, Including Salvage Interventions for Local Regrowth</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><col align="left" width="1*" span="1"/><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1">Initial Response</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1">n (%)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1">W&#x00026;W Without Regrowth</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1">W&#x00026;W With Regrowth</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1">LE</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1">LAR</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1">APR</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1" axis="1">Refused Surgery</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">cCR</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">33 (51.6)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">27 (1 LWD)</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">4</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3 1 pCR 1 pT1Nx (salvage) 1 pT2Nx (salvage)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 1 pCR</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 1 pT1N1 (salvage)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 1 regrowth (1 DOO)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Near-cCR</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">13 (20.3)</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">4</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">3</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3 1 pCR 1 pT1 1 pT2</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 1 pCR 2 pT1N0 (2 salvage) 3 pT2N0 (1 salvage)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Non-cCR</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">18 (28.1)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2 delayed cCR<xref rid="T3fn1" ref-type="table-fn">*</xref> (initially refuse surgery)</td><td align="char" char="." rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2 2 pT2 (1 LWD)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8 2 pCR 1 pTisN0 4 pT2N0</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5 1 pCR 1 ypT2N0 3 ypT3N0 (1 LWD)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 1 residual tumors (1 DOD)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Overall [n (%)]</td><td align="char" char="(" rowspan="1" colspan="1">64 (100)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">33 (51.6)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02014;</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8 (12.5)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15 (23.4)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6 (9.4)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2 (3.1)</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="T3fn1"><label>*</label><p>One patient who was diagnosed with delayed cCR had grade 2 to 3 adenoma close to the residual scar at the last follow-up.</p></fn><fn fn-type="other"><p>DOD indicates died of disease; DOO, died of other cause; LWD, living with disease.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><p>When considering cCR/near-cCR without regrowth in W&#x00026;W or ypCR after surgery in cCR/near-cCR patients as the true positive diagnosis, the accuracy of cCR and cCR+near-cCR diagnoses using the Memorial Sloan-Kettering Cancer Center criteria in the present study was 0.766 and 0.750, respectively. The diagnostic efficiency data for cCR and cCR+near-cCR are shown in Appendix Table a2 (Supplemental File, Supplemental Digital Content 2, <ext-link xlink:href="http://links.lww.com/SLA/D908" ext-link-type="uri">http://links.lww.com/SLA/D908</ext-link>).</p></sec><sec><title>OPR and Survival</title><p>The median follow-up duration was 39.5 months (interquartile range: 4.3&#x02013;67.9 months). The 3-year OPR was 67.2% (95% CI: 55.6%&#x02013;78.8%) when estimated from the TME-free survival curve. No difference was found in the 3-year OPR between cT2 and T3a/b subgroups (73.3% vs 65.3%, <italic toggle="yes">P</italic>=0.492).</p><p>The estimated 3-year stoma-free survival rate was 82.7% (95% CI: 73.5%&#x02013;91.9%); 6 patients had a permanent colostomy after APR; in 15 patients who underwent LAR, 4 did not have ileostomy closure for anastomotic stricture or dehiscence, 1 patient had to undergo a colostomy for pelvic infection following ileostomy closure. The estimated 3-year cancer-specific survival and NR-DFS were 96.6% (95% CI: 92.1%&#x02013;100%) and 92.2% (95% CI: 85.5%&#x02013;98.9%), respectively. The TME, NR-DFS, and stoma-free survival KM curves are shown in Figure <xref rid="F4" ref-type="fig">4</xref>. No local recurrence occurred in patients who underwent LE, LAR, or APR. One cCR patient with regrowth refused surgery and died of acute myocardial infarction. In 3 non-cCR patients who refused initial surgery, 2 were identified to have delayed cCR during the follow-up period, and 1 died of multiple metastases. One cCR patient who received W&#x00026;W, 1 non-cCR patient who received LE (ypT2Nx), and 1 non-cCR patient who received APR (ypT3N0) had lung metastases, which were managed using palliative chemotherapy, and they continue to live with the disease. The overall rate of metastasis was 6.3% (4/64). The metastasis rates were 2.2% (1/46) in cCR/near-cCR cases and 16.7% (3/18) in non-cCR cases, respectively.</p><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>FIGURE 4</label><caption><p>The KM curves of TME-free survival, NR-DFS, and stoma-free survival. The estimated 3-year OPR is 67.2% (95% CI: 55.6%&#x02013;78.8%), NR-DFS is 92.2% (95% CI: 85.5%&#x02013;98.9%), and stoma-free survival is 82.7% (95% CI: 73.5%&#x02013;91.9%).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="sla-277-0647-g004" position="float"/></fig></sec></sec><sec sec-type="discussion"><title>DISCUSSION</title><p>This study tested the efficacy and safety of intentional W&#x00026;W and organ preservation surgery following intensive neoadjuvant treatment in patients with MRI-defined low-risk rectal cancer. Following IMRT and consolidation CAPEOX, these patients achieved high initial cCR/near-cCR rate and OPR, with acceptable oncologic outcomes.</p><p>Accidental W&#x00026;W was initially noted in the patient subgroup with locally advanced rectal cancer, but the efficacy and safety of W&#x00026;W as an alternative treatment to maximally benefit organ preservation has been proved.<xref rid="R3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>,<xref rid="R4" ref-type="bibr">4</xref>,<xref rid="R8" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>,<xref rid="R9" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>,<xref rid="R16" ref-type="bibr">16</xref> However, the low rate of patients with typical cCR features (5%&#x02013;8%), which is much lower than the ypCR rate of 15% to 30%, limits the wider use of W&#x00026;W in routine clinical management.<xref rid="R10" ref-type="bibr">10</xref>,<xref rid="R17" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>,<xref rid="R18" ref-type="bibr">18</xref> Tumors with higher T3 substages cannot often be determined as having cCR after a good response because of the obvious ulceration and residual thickening of the rectal wall.<xref rid="R10" ref-type="bibr">10</xref> Furthermore, radiosensitivity increases with less tumor infiltration and a tumor with a larger volume (or tumor length and circumferential extent) has a very low expected cCR rate.<xref rid="R19" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>,<xref rid="R20" ref-type="bibr">20</xref> MRI can distinguish a T2 or T3a/T3b tumor substage, which has a low tumor burden with extramural invasion &#x0003c;5&#x000a0;mm.<xref rid="R21" ref-type="bibr">21</xref> The premise that this subgroup has excellent outcome following direct TME makes surgical treatment preponderant; however, these subgroup can be also considered suitable for receiving intensive NCRT when the demands of organ preservation are high.<xref rid="R22" ref-type="bibr">22</xref> Moreover, baseline MRI can preclude systemic risk factors like positive extramural vascular invasion or MRF involvement, to facilitate W&#x00026;W with a low rate of distant failure.<xref rid="R7" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>,<xref rid="R23" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="R25" ref-type="bibr">25</xref>
</p><p>For the abovementioned reasons, the lack of published data on intentional W&#x00026;W for MRI-defined low-risk tumors without risk factors impelled us to design this study.<xref rid="R26" ref-type="bibr">26</xref> We aimed to maximize the possibility of reaching cCR while avoiding definitive organ resection by TNT, similar to the neoadjuvant regimen in the Timing trial and the Sao Paulo study.<xref rid="R22" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>,<xref rid="R27" ref-type="bibr">27</xref> The single-arm design of this single-center study was adopted for the following reasons: first, this phase 2 trial was conducted to prospectively observe the efficacy of intentional W&#x00026;W, and a single arm was considered sufficient when using MRI to objectively select patients; second, a randomized trial after the occurrence of cCR/near-cCR is difficult when considering patients&#x02019; preference;<xref rid="R28" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>,<xref rid="R29" ref-type="bibr">29</xref> third, an ideal design should include 1 arm of intentional W&#x00026;W and organ preservation surgery that is compared with another arm of direct surgery or a historical control group; meanwhile, the data of the former arm should be tested in advance.<xref rid="R30" ref-type="bibr">30</xref> Finally, decision-making through MDT discussion in a single-center can minimize the intersurgeon and intrasurgeon variations in response assessment.<xref rid="R31" ref-type="bibr">31</xref>
</p><p>The findings of this study consolidate the high possibility of achieving empirical cCR in patients who have small or early-stage rectal cancers, determined through baseline MRI as an objective radiological assessment. After completion of TNT, more than half of the patients can be treated with W&#x00026;W and two third can successfully have organ preservation. Regardless of the high initial cCR/near-cCR rate, the present strategy can improve organ preservation, either through intentional W&#x00026;W or LE. The OPR is very close to the result from the GRECCAR-2 trial (around 65%). However, the high weight of W&#x00026;W cases in this study makes this whole strategy more attractive when LE-related complications can be avoided. The initial cCR rate of T2 and cT3a/b patients is comparable to previous reports.<xref rid="R11" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>,<xref rid="R22" ref-type="bibr">22</xref> The overall cCR/near-cCR rate is approaching the treatment outcomes of anal squamous cancer having nearly 80% cCR rate.<xref rid="R32" ref-type="bibr">32</xref> The regrowth rate of cCR patients is lower than the reported regrowth rate of 25%, which may be attributed to the selection of low-risk cancers. The distant failure rate is also low for the same reason.<xref rid="R3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>,<xref rid="R4" ref-type="bibr">4</xref>,<xref rid="R7" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>&#x02013;<xref rid="R9" ref-type="bibr">9</xref> Another finding is that LE after TNT is a safe alternative for organ preservation, both as initial or salvage treatment.</p><p>Although oncologic results were not compromised in this study, more long-term data of oncologic or functional outcomes are still needed. At least 5-year survival data is mandatory to preclude late tumor recurrence after TNT and further verify oncologic safety. In the foreseeable future, prospective randomized trials like the STAR-TREC trial will hopefully provide more evidence for organ preservation strategy in rectal cancers.<xref rid="R33" ref-type="bibr">33</xref> We also designed another multi-center phase 2 trial (Beijing-R01 trial, NCT04405206) with a similar design to the present trial, aiming to test the intentional W&#x00026;W and organ preservation strategy in 5 high-volume centers in the Beijing Area.</p><p>This study has several limitations. The single-arm phase 2 trial design with a small sample size and relatively short follow-up time limits the evidence level of results. Furthermore, we used an aggressive TNT regimen but did not assess the early response to treatment and, therefore, may have ignored some potential cases with early cCR who could have avoided further administration of consolidation CAPEOX. Moreover, we did not set a threshold of the baseline tumor size or the scale of annular invasion, which is critical in making a decision of LE after TNT. Finally, the data of long-term anorectal function and quality of life are not reported in this article. Nevertheless, more optimal schedules of intentional W&#x00026;W and organ preservation surgery for this radiosensitive subgroup would be further explored in future trials.</p></sec><sec sec-type="conclusions"><title>CONCLUSIONS</title><p>In summary, chemoradiotherapy plus consolidation CAPEOX for MRI-defined low-risk rectal cancer can lead to high rates of organ preservation through intentional W&#x00026;W or LE. The oncologic safety of this strategy should be further tested.</p></sec><sec sec-type="supplementary-material"><title>Supplementary Material</title><fig id="s001" position="anchor"><media xlink:href="sla-277-0647-s001.docx"/></fig><fig id="s002" position="anchor"><media xlink:href="sla-277-0647-s002.docx"/></fig></sec></body><back><ack><title>ACKNOWLEDGMENTS</title><p>The authors thank Ting-Ting Sun for statistics support; Yuan-Lian Xu and Yue Zhang for case management support; Dr Yun-Feng Yao, Dr Yi-Fan Peng, and Dr Tian-Cheng Zhan for providing cases in the GI Surgery Department; Professor Yong Cai, Dr Yong-Heng Li, Dr Jian-Hao Geng, and Dr Shuai Li for their works in the Radiation Oncology Department; Professor Qi Wu, Dr Jing Wang, and Dr Peng Yuan for their works in the Endoscopy Center Department; Professor Yang Ke from Key Laboratory of Carcinogenesis and Translational Research for sharing her experience of publishing articles in <italic toggle="yes">Annals of Surgery</italic>; and Professor Jia-Fu Ji for his leadership as the president of Peking University Cancer Hospital and the Chief of the GI Surgery Center.</p></ack><fn-group><fn fn-type="other"><p>L.W., X.-Y.Z., and Y.-M.Z. contributed equally as first authors.</p></fn><fn fn-type="supported-by"><p>Supported by The Beijing Municipal Science &#x00026; Technology Commission Capital Clinical Research Special Fund (Z151100004015105); National Natural Science Foundation of China (81773214); Beijing Hospitals Authority Clinical Medicine Development of Special Funding Support, code: (ZYLX202116).</p></fn><fn fn-type="COI-statement"><p>The authors report no conflicts of interest.</p></fn><fn fn-type="other"><p>Supplemental Digital Content is available for this article. Direct URL citations appear in the printed text and are provided in the HTML and PDF versions of this article on the journal's website, <ext-link xlink:href="www.annalsofsurgery.com" ext-link-type="uri">www.annalsofsurgery.com</ext-link>.</p></fn></fn-group><ref-list><title>REFERENCES</title><ref id="R1"><label>1</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sebag-Montefiore</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Stephens</surname><given-names>RJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Steele</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><etal/></person-group>. <article-title>Preoperative radiotherapy versus selective postoperative chemoradiotherapy in patients with rectal cancer (MRC CR07 and NCIC-CTG C016): a multicentre, randomised trial</article-title>. <source>Lancet</source>. <yea